WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.840
Hardening begins with knowing what systems exist.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:08.820
A useful inventory includes workstations, laptops, servers, virtual machines,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.040
cloud instances, containers, appliances, administrative systems, lab systems,

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.460
and endpoint devices that may not be centrally managed yet.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:24.360
It should record the system owner, business purpose, operating system, role, location,

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:29.380
exposure, installed software where practical, and whether the system is supported.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.080
Unknown systems cannot be hardened reliably.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.860
Ownership turns discovery into accountability.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.240
Every system should have a team or person responsible for decisions about configuration,

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.140
patching, access, backup, and retirement.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.000
Without an owner, remediation work stalls.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.540
A server may need a patch, an endpoint protection agent may be unhealthy,

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.640
or a remote access exception may be too broad,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.260
but no one can approve or schedule the fix.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.400
Hardening requires someone who understands the system's purpose and risk.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.180
Classification helps prioritize effort.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:08.680
Production systems, development systems, internet-facing systems, internal systems,

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.220
systems with sensitive data, administrative workstations, ordinary user endpoints,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.680
and low-risk lab devices do not all need the same urgency or controls.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.380
Classification helps the team decide where stricter baselines, faster patching,

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:27.080
stronger monitoring, encryption, or tighter access controls are most important.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.540
Inventory is a living control.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.400
New cloud instances appear, laptops are reassigned, containers are rebuilt,

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.940
software is installed, and old systems remain online longer than planned.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.040
Regular inventory review helps teams find shadow systems, unsupported software,

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.460
missing endpoint protection, and systems with no owner.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.840
It also gives hardening work a safer starting point because teams can make decisions from

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.900
evidence rather than guesses.
